  <h2><a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2023/02/15/">CRT-free in 2023=
: tips and tricks</a></h2>
  <time datetime=3D"2023-02-15">
    February 15, 2023
  </time>
  <div class=3D"print-only url">
    nullprogram.com/blog/2023/02/15/
  </div>

  <p>Seven years ago I wrote about <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/=
2016/01/31/">=E2=80=9Cfreestanding=E2=80=9D Windows executables</a>.
After an additional seven years of practical experience both writing and
distributing such programs, half using <a href=3D"https://github.com/skeeto=
/w64devkit">a custom-built toolchain</a>,
it=E2=80=99s time to revisit these cabalistic incantations and otherwise sc=
ant
details. I=E2=80=99ve tweaked my older article over the years as I=E2=80=99=
ve learned, but
this is a full replacement and does not assumes you=E2=80=99ve read it. The=
 <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2023/02/11/">=E2=80=9Cwhy=E2=80=9D
has been covered</a> and the focus will be on the =E2=80=9Chow=E2=80=9D. Bo=
th the GNU
and MSVC toolchains will be considered.</p>

<p>I no longer call these =E2=80=9Cfreestanding=E2=80=9D programs since tha=
t term is, at
best, <a href=3D"https://github.com/ipxe/ipxe/commit/e8393c372">inaccurate<=
/a>. In fact, we will be actively avoiding GCC
features associated with that label. Instead I call these <em>CRT-free</em>
programs, where CRT stands for the <em>C runtime</em> the Windows-oriented =
term
for <em>libc</em>. This term communicates both intent and scope.</p>

<h3 id=3D"entry-point">Entry point</h3>

<p>You should already know that <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlight=
er-rouge">main</code> is not the program=E2=80=99s entry point, but
a C application=E2=80=99s entry point. The CRT provides the entry point, wh=
ere it
initializes the CRT, including <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2022=
/02/18/">parsing command line options</a>, then
calls the application=E2=80=99s <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlight=
er-rouge">main</code>. The real entry point doesn=E2=80=99t have a name.
It=E2=80=99s just the address of the function to be called by the loader wi=
thout
arguments.</p>

<p>You might naively assume you could continue using the name <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">main</code> and tell
the linker to use it as the entry point. You would be wrong. <strong>Avoid =
the
name <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">main</code>!</str=
ong> It has a special meaning in C gets special treatment. Using
it without a conventional CRT will confuse your tools an may cause build
issues.</p>

<p>While you can use almost any other name you like, the conventional names
are <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">mainCRTStartup</co=
de> (console subsystem) and <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-r=
ouge">WinMainCRTStartup</code> (windows
subsystem). It=E2=80=99s easy to remember: Append <code class=3D"language-p=
laintext highlighter-rouge">CRTStartup</code> to the name you=E2=80=99d
use in a normal CRT-linking application. I strongly recommend using these
names because it reduces friction. Your tools are already familiar with
them, so you won=E2=80=99t need to do anything special.</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">int</span> <span class=3D"nf">m=
ainCRTStartup</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</span=
><span class=3D"p">);</span>     <span class=3D"c1">// console subsystem</s=
pan>
<span class=3D"kt">int</span> <span class=3D"nf">WinMainCRTStartup</span><s=
pan class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</span><span class=3D"p">);<=
/span>  <span class=3D"c1">// windows subsystem</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>The MSVC linker documentation says the entry point uses the <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__stdcall</code>
calling convention. <del>Ignore this and <strong>do not use <code class=3D"=
language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__stdcall</code> for your
entry point!</strong></del> Since entry points may take no arguments, there=
 is no
practical difference from the <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter=
-rouge">__cdecl</code> calling convention, so it matters
little. <del>Rather, the goal is to avoid <code class=3D"language-plaintext=
 highlighter-rouge">__stdcall</code> <em>function decorations</em>.
In particular, the GNU linker <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter=
-rouge">--entry</code> option does not understand them,
nor can it find decorated entry points on its own. If you use <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__stdcall</code>,
then the 32-bit GNU linker will silently (!) choose the beginning of your
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.text</code> section a=
s the entry point.</del> (This bug was fixed in Binutils
2.42, released January 2024. <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-=
rouge">__stdcall</code> entry points now link correctly.)</p>

<p>If you=E2=80=99re using C++, then of course you will also need to use <c=
ode class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">extern "C"</code>
so that it=E2=80=99s not name-mangled. Otherwise the results are similarly =
bad.</p>

<p>If using <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-fwhole-pr=
ogram</code>, you will need to mark your entry point as
externally visible for GCC so that it knows its an entry point. While
linkers are familiar with conventional entry point names, GCC the
<em>compiler</em> is not. Normally you do not need to worry about this.</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">__attribute</span><span class=3D=
"p">((</span><span class=3D"n">externally_visible</span><span class=3D"p">)=
)</span>  <span class=3D"c1">// for -fwhole-program</span>
<span class=3D"kt">int</span> <span class=3D"nf">mainCRTStartup</span><span=
 class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</span><span class=3D"p">)</spa=
n>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"k">return</span> <span class=3D"mi">0</span><span class=
=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>The entry point returns <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-ro=
uge">int</code>. <em>If there are no other threads</em> then the
process will exit with the returned value as its exit status. In practice
this is only useful for console programs. Windows subsystem programs have
threads started automatically, without warning, and it=E2=80=99s almost cer=
tain
your main thread is not the last thread. You probably want to use
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ExitProcess</code> or =
even <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">TerminateProcess<=
/code> instead of returning. The latter
exits more abruptly and can avoid issues with certain subsystems, like
DirectSound, not shutting down gracefully: It doesn=E2=80=99t even let them=
 try.</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">int</span> <span class=3D"nf">W=
inMainCRTStartup</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</s=
pan><span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"c1">// ...</span>
    <span class=3D"n">TerminateProcess</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><spa=
n class=3D"n">GetCurrentProcess</span><span class=3D"p">(),</span> <span cl=
ass=3D"mi">0</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<h3 id=3D"compilation">Compilation</h3>

<p>Starting with the GNU toolchain, you have two ways to get into =E2=80=9C=
CRT-free
mode=E2=80=9D: <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-nostar=
tfiles</code> and <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-nos=
tdlib</code>. The former is more dummy-proof,
and it=E2=80=99s what I use in build documentation. The latter can be a mor=
e
complicated, but when it succeeds you get guarantees about the result. I
use it in build scripts I intend to run myself, which I want to fail if
they don=E2=80=99t do exactly what I expect. To illustrate, consider this t=
rivial
program:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"cp">#include</span> <span class=3D"=
cpf">&lt;windows.h&gt;</span><span class=3D"cp">
</span>
<span class=3D"kt">int</span> <span class=3D"nf">mainCRTStartup</span><span=
 class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</span><span class=3D"p">)</spa=
n>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"n">ExitProcess</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span cla=
ss=3D"mi">0</span><span class=3D"p">);</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>This program uses <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">E=
xitProcess</code> from <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"=
>kernel32.dll</code>. Compiling is easy:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc -nostartfiles example.c
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>The <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-nostartfiles</=
code> prevents it from linking the CRT entry point, but it
still implicitly passes other =E2=80=9Cstandard=E2=80=9D linker flags, incl=
uding libraries
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-lmingw32</code> and <=
code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-lkernel32</code>. Prog=
rams can use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">kernel32.=
dll</code> functions
without explicitly linking that DLL. But, hey, isn=E2=80=99t <code class=3D=
"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-lmingw32</code> the CRT,
the thing we=E2=80=99re avoiding? It is, but it wasn=E2=80=99t actually lin=
ked because the
program didn=E2=80=99t reference it.</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ objdump -p a.exe | grep -Fi .dll
        DLL Name: KERNEL32.dll
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>However, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-nostdlib<=
/code> does not pass any of these libraries, so you need to
do so explicitly.</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc -nostdlib example.c -lkernel32
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>The MSVC toolchain behaves a little like <code class=3D"language-plainte=
xt highlighter-rouge">-nostartfiles</code>, not linking a
CRT unless you need it, semi-automatically. However, you=E2=80=99ll need to=
 list
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">kernel32.dll</code> an=
d tell it which subsystem you=E2=80=99re using.</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cl example.c /link /subsystem:console ke=
rnel32.lib
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>However, MSVC has a handy little feature to list these arguments in the
source file.</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"cp">#ifdef _MSC_VER
</span>  <span class=3D"cp">#pragma comment(linker, "/subsystem:console")
</span>  <span class=3D"cp">#pragma comment(lib, "kernel32.lib")
#endif
</span></code></pre></div></div>

<p>This information must go somewhere, and I prefer the source file rather
than a build script. Then anyone can point MSVC at the source without
worrying about options.</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cl example.c
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>I try to make all my Windows programs so simply built.</p>

<h3 id=3D"stack-probes">Stack probes</h3>

<p>On Windows, it=E2=80=99s expected that stacks will commit dynamically. T=
hat is,
the stack is merely <em>reserved</em> address space, and it=E2=80=99s only =
committed when
the stack actually grows into it. This made sense 30 years ago as a memory
saving technique, but today it no longer makes sense. However, programs
are still built to use this mechanism.</p>

<p>To function properly, programs must touch each stack page for the first
time in order. Normally that=E2=80=99s not an issue, but if your stack fram=
e
exceeds the page size, there=E2=80=99s a chance it might step over a page. =
When a
function has a large stack frame, GCC inserts a call to a =E2=80=9Cstack pr=
obe=E2=80=9D in
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">libgcc</code> that tou=
ches its pages in the prologue. It=E2=80=99s not unlike <a href=3D"https://=
nullprogram.com/blog/2017/06/21/">stack
clash protection</a>.</p>

<p>For example, if I have a 4kiB local variable:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">int</span> <span class=3D"nf">m=
ainCRTStartup</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</span=
><span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"kt">char</span> <span class=3D"n">buf</span><span class=
=3D"p">[</span><span class=3D"mi">1</span><span class=3D"o">&lt;&lt;</span>=
<span class=3D"mi">12</span><span class=3D"p">]</span> <span class=3D"o">=
=3D</span> <span class=3D"p">{</span><span class=3D"mi">0</span><span class=
=3D"p">};</span>
    <span class=3D"k">return</span> <span class=3D"mi">0</span><span class=
=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>When I compile with <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"=
>-nostdlib</code>:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc -nostdlib example.c
ld: ... undefined reference to `___chkstk_ms'
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>It=E2=80=99s trying to link the CRT stack probe. You can disable this be=
havior
with <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-mno-stack-arg-pr=
obe</code>.</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc -mno-stack-arg-probe -nostdlib exampl=
e.c
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Or you can just link <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge=
">-lgcc</code> to provide a definition:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc -nostdlib example.c -lgcc
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Had you used <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-nosta=
rtfiles</code>, you wouldn=E2=80=99t have noticed because it passes
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-lgcc</code> automatic=
ally. It=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9Cdummy-proof=E2=80=9D because this sort of issue=
 goes
away before it comes up, though for the same reason it=E2=80=99s harder to =
tell
exactly what went into a program.</p>

<p>If you disable the probe altogether =E2=80=94 my preference =E2=80=94 yo=
u=E2=80=99ve only solved
the linker problem, but the underlying stack commit problem remains and
your program may crash. You can solve that by telling the linker to ask
the loader to commit a larger stack up front rather than grow it at run
time. Say, 2MiB:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc -mno-stack-arg-probe -Xlinker --stack=
=3D0x200000,0x200000 example.c
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Of course, I wish that this was simply the default behavior because it=
=E2=80=99s
far more sensible! A much better option is to avoid large stack frames in
the first place. Allocate locals larger than, say, 1KiB in a scratch arena
instead of on the stack.</p>

<p>MSVC doesn=E2=80=99t have <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-=
rouge">libgcc</code> of course, but it still generates stack probes
both for growing the stack and for security checks. The latter requires
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">kernel32.dll</code>, s=
o if I compile the same program with MSVC, I get a bunch
of linker failures:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cl example.c /link /subsystem:console
... unresolved external symbol __imp_RtlCaptureContext ...
... and 7 more ...
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Using <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">/Gs1000000000=
</code> turns off the stack probes, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highl=
ighter-rouge">/GS-</code> turns off the
checks, <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">/stack</code> =
commits a larger stack:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cl /GS- /Gs1000000000 example.c /link
     /subsystem:console /stack:0x200000,200000
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Though, as before, better to avoid large stack frames in the first place=
.</p>

<h3 id=3D"built-in-functions-ugh">Built-in functions=E2=80=A6 ugh</h3>

<p>The three major C and C++ compilers =E2=80=94 GCC, MSVC, Clang =E2=80=94=
 share a common,
evil weakness: =E2=80=9Cbuilt-in=E2=80=9D functions. <em>No matter what</em=
>, they each assume
you will supply definitions for standard string functions at link time,
particularly <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">memset</c=
ode> and <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">memcpy</code>=
. They do this no matter how many
=E2=80=9Cseriously now, do not use standard C functions=E2=80=9D options yo=
u pass. When
you don=E2=80=99t link a CRT, you may need to define them yourself.</p>

<p>With GCC there=E2=80=99s a catch: it will transform your <code class=3D"=
language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">memset</code> definition =E2=80=94
that is, <em>in a function named <code class=3D"language-plaintext highligh=
ter-rouge">memset</code></em> =E2=80=94 into a call to itself. After
all, it looks an awful lot like <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlight=
er-rouge">memset</code>! This typically manifests as an
infinite loop. <strong>Use <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-ro=
uge">-fno-builtin</code> to prevent GCC from mis-compiling
built-in functions.</strong></p>

<p>Even with <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-fno-buil=
tin</code>, both GCC and Clang will continue inserting calls
to built-in functions elsewhere. For example, making an especially large
local variable (and using <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rou=
ge">volatile</code> to prevent it from being optimized
out):</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"kt">int</span> <span class=3D"nf">m=
ainCRTStartup</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</span=
><span class=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"k">volatile</span> <span class=3D"kt">char</span> <span =
class=3D"n">buf</span><span class=3D"p">[</span><span class=3D"mi">1</span>=
<span class=3D"o">&lt;&lt;</span><span class=3D"mi">14</span><span class=3D=
"p">]</span> <span class=3D"o">=3D</span> <span class=3D"p">{</span><span c=
lass=3D"mi">0</span><span class=3D"p">};</span>
    <span class=3D"k">return</span> <span class=3D"mi">0</span><span class=
=3D"p">;</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>As of this writing, the latest GCC and Clang will generate a <code class=
=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">memset</code> call
despite <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">-fno-builtin</=
code>:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc -mno-stack-arg-probe -fno-builtin -no=
stdlib example.c
ld: ... undefined reference to `memset' ...
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>To be absolutely pure, you will need to address this in just about any
non-trivial program. On the other hand, <code class=3D"language-plaintext h=
ighlighter-rouge">-nostartfiles</code> will grab a
definition from <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">msvcrt=
.dll</code> for you:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc -nostartfiles example.c
$ objdump -p a.exe | grep -Fi .dll
        DLL Name: msvcrt.dll
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>To be clear, <em>this is a completely legitimate and pragmatic route!</e=
m> You
get the benefits of both worlds: the CRT is still out of the way, but
there=E2=80=99s also no hassle from misbehaving compilers. If this sounds l=
ike a
good deal, then do it! (For on-lookers feeling smug: there is no such
easy, general solution for this problem on Linux.)</p>

<p>When you write your own definitions, I suggest putting each definition i=
n
its own section so that they can be discarded via <code class=3D"language-p=
laintext highlighter-rouge">-Wl,--gc-sections</code> when
unused:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">__attribute</span><span class=3D=
"p">((</span><span class=3D"n">section</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><spa=
n class=3D"s">".text.memset"</span><span class=3D"p">)))</span>
<span class=3D"kt">void</span> <span class=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"nf"=
>memset</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</span> <spa=
n class=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"n">d</span><span class=3D"p">,</span> =
<span class=3D"kt">int</span> <span class=3D"n">c</span><span class=3D"p">,=
</span> <span class=3D"kt">size_t</span> <span class=3D"n">n</span><span cl=
ass=3D"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"c1">// ...</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>So far, for all three compilers, I=E2=80=99ve only needed to provide def=
initions
for <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">memset</code> and =
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">memcpy</code>.</p>

<h3 id=3D"stack-alignment-on-32-bit-x86">Stack alignment on 32-bit x86</h3>

<p>GCC expects a 16-byte aligned stack and generates code accordingly. Such
is dictated by the x64 ABI, so that=E2=80=99s a given on 64-bit Windows. Ho=
wever,
the x86 ABIs only guarantee 4-byte alignment. If no care is taken to deal
with it, there will likely be unaligned loads. Some may not be valid (e.g.
SIMD) leading to a crash. UBSan disapproves, too. Fortunately there=E2=80=
=99s a
function attribute for this:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">__attribute</span><span class=3D=
"p">((</span><span class=3D"n">force_align_arg_pointer</span><span class=3D=
"p">))</span>
<span class=3D"kt">int</span> <span class=3D"nf">mainCRTStartup</span><span=
 class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</span><span class=3D"p">)</spa=
n>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"c1">// ...</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>GCC will now align the stack in this function=E2=80=99s prologue. Adjust=
ment is
only necessary at entry points, as GCC will maintain alignment through its
own frames. This includes <em>all</em> entry points, not just the program e=
ntry
point, particularly thread start functions. Rule of thumb for i686 GCC:
<strong>If <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">WINAPI</cod=
e> or <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">__stdcall</code>=
 appears in a definition, the stack likely
requires alignment</strong>.</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"n">__attribute</span><span class=3D=
"p">((</span><span class=3D"n">force_align_arg_pointer</span><span class=3D=
"p">))</span>
<span class=3D"n">DWORD</span> <span class=3D"n">WINAPI</span> <span class=
=3D"nf">mythread</span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</s=
pan> <span class=3D"o">*</span><span class=3D"n">arg</span><span class=3D"p=
">)</span>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"c1">// ...</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>It=E2=80=99s harmless to use this attribute on x64. The prologue will ju=
st be a
smidge larger. If you=E2=80=99re worried about it, use <code class=3D"langu=
age-plaintext highlighter-rouge">#ifdef __i686__</code> to limit
it to 32-bit builds.</p>

<h3 id=3D"putting-it-all-together">Putting it all together</h3>

<p>If I=E2=80=99ve written a graphical application with <code class=3D"lang=
uage-plaintext highlighter-rouge">WinMainCRTStartup</code>, used
large stack frames, marked my entry point as externally visible, plan to
support 32-bit builds, and defined a couple of needed string functions, my
optimal entry point may look something like:</p>

<div class=3D"language-c highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight"><pre c=
lass=3D"highlight"><code><span class=3D"cp">#ifdef __GNUC__
</span><span class=3D"n">__attribute</span><span class=3D"p">((</span><span=
 class=3D"n">externally_visible</span><span class=3D"p">))</span>
<span class=3D"cp">#endif
#ifdef __i686__
</span><span class=3D"n">__attribute</span><span class=3D"p">((</span><span=
 class=3D"n">force_align_arg_pointer</span><span class=3D"p">))</span>
<span class=3D"cp">#endif
</span><span class=3D"kt">int</span> <span class=3D"nf">WinMainCRTStartup</=
span><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"kt">void</span><span class=3D=
"p">)</span>
<span class=3D"p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"c1">// ...</span>
<span class=3D"p">}</span>
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Then my =E2=80=9Coptimize all the things=E2=80=9D release build may look=
 something like:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc -O3 -fno-builtin -Wl,--gc-sections -s=
 -nostdlib -mwindows
     -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -o app.exe app.c -lkernel32
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Or with MSVC:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cl /O2 /GS- app.c /link kernel32.lib /su=
bsystem:windows
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Or if I=E2=80=99m taking it easy maybe just:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cc -O3 -fno-builtin -s -nostartfiles -mw=
indows -o app.exe app.c
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Or with MSVC (linker flags in source):</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>$ cl /O2 app.c
</code></pre></div></div>



